# .clinerules — Full Ruleset
# Place in project root (or split into .clinerules/01-*.md files). Adjust the PROJECT section at the bottom.

## 0. How to Use These Rules
- Priority order when rules conflict: (1) explicit user instruction in the current task, (2) safety/security rules, (3) project conventions already in the repo, (4) this file.
- If a user instruction conflicts with safety or data-integrity rules, say so and propose a safer alternative before proceeding.
- Never claim something works, passes, or is deployed unless it was actually run or verified. State what was NOT verified.

## 1. Workflow: Explore → Plan → Implement → Verify → Report
1. Explore: read relevant files, imports, types, tests, configs, and recent git history. Search by symbol before assuming. Never invent APIs, flags, file paths, or package versions.
2. Plan: for anything non-trivial, write a short plan (goal, files, order, risks, verification). Ask ONE focused question only if blocked or if the action is risky or irreversible.
3. Implement: smallest correct change, in small steps, following existing patterns.
4. Verify: run type-check, lint, tests, and build as defined by the project. Fix what you broke.
5. Report: summarize what changed, which files, how it was verified, what remains, and any risks or assumptions.

## 2. Large Code & Large Changes (applies if a change > ~500 characters, a file > ~500 lines, or 3+ files are touched)
### 2.1 Plan before editing
- List files to touch, the order, and the verification step for each stage.
- Get confirmation before: public API changes, DB schema changes, dependency upgrades, large refactors, deletions.

### 2.2 Edit surgically
- Use targeted search/replace or diff edits. Do NOT regenerate an entire file to change a few lines.
- In files over ~500 lines, read only the needed ranges (by function/class name), and edit only those blocks.
- NEVER write placeholders such as "// ... existing code ...", "# rest unchanged", or "TODO: implement" in place of real code that exists or was requested.
- After several edits in the same file, re-read the region before the next edit (line numbers and context shift).
- Preserve unrelated code, comments, formatting, and whitespace. No mass reformatting inside a feature change.

### 2.3 Split big things into small things
- Files > ~500 lines: propose a split by responsibility (components / hooks / services / repositories / utils / types / constants).
- Functions < ~40 lines, one responsibility. React components < ~200 lines. Classes with one reason to change.
- Do refactors/moves as a separate step from behavior changes so each diff is reviewable.
- After moving code: update imports/exports, grep for old paths and symbols, run type-check.

### 2.4 Keep the project green
- One logical change → verify → next change. Never leave the build broken between steps.
- Each step should be committable with a clear conventional message.

### 2.5 Long outputs
- If output would be too long, deliver in parts (per file or per section), state what is done and what remains.
- Never silently skip part of a requested change. List anything deferred explicitly.

## 3. Context & Memory Management
- Prefer reading specific files/ranges over dumping whole directories. Use grep/search to locate code.
- Summarize findings before moving on so context stays small and decisions are explicit.
- For multi-session or long tasks, keep a short progress note (e.g., docs/progress.md or a task checklist) with: done, in progress, next, open questions.
- Don't re-read unchanged files repeatedly; don't paste huge logs, show the relevant lines only.

## 4. Code Quality (all languages)
- Names express intent; avoid abbreviations and generic names (`data`, `tmp`, `handle`).
- Comments explain WHY, not WHAT. Delete dead code and commented-out blocks.
- Early returns, flat control flow, max ~3 nesting levels. Prefer pure functions and immutability.
- No magic numbers/strings → constants/enums/config.
- DRY with judgment: extract on the 3rd duplication, avoid premature abstraction.
- Explicit error handling: never swallow exceptions; return or throw typed, meaningful errors; add context to messages without leaking secrets.
- Validate all external input at the boundary (HTTP, queue messages, env vars, files, webhooks, LLM output).
- Prefer composition over inheritance. Depend on interfaces, inject dependencies.
- Keep functions side-effect-light; isolate I/O at the edges.
- Don't add dependencies for trivial tasks. If adding one, state why, check maintenance/license/size, and pin the version.

## 5. TypeScript / JavaScript
- `strict: true`. No `any` (use `unknown` + narrowing, generics, or proper types). No `@ts-ignore`/`@ts-expect-error` without a justifying comment.
- Prefer `type` imports, discriminated unions, `satisfies`, readonly where possible, exhaustive `switch` checks.
- Validate at boundaries with Zod (or class-validator in NestJS) and infer types from schemas.
- `async/await` everywhere; no floating promises; use `Promise.all` for independent work; handle rejections.
- Avoid mutation of function arguments; avoid global state; avoid deep prop drilling (use context or state libs sparingly).
- Use the repo's ESLint/Prettier config. Don't modify lint/format/tsconfig rules without asking.

### 5.1 React / Next.js
- Function components + hooks. Keep components focused; extract custom hooks for logic; colocate small helpers.
- Next.js App Router: default to Server Components; add "use client" only where needed; fetch data on the server; use route handlers/server actions with validation and auth checks.
- Avoid unnecessary useEffect (derive state, use event handlers, use data-fetching libs). Always include correct dependency arrays.
- Accessibility: semantic HTML, labels, keyboard navigation, alt text, sufficient contrast, focus management.
- Performance: lazy-load heavy components, use next/image, avoid large client bundles, memoize only after measuring.
- Handle loading, empty, and error states for every async UI.
- Styling: follow the project's system (Tailwind/CSS modules). No inline style sprawl; use design tokens.

### 5.2 Node / Express / NestJS
- Thin controllers/routes, business logic in services, data access in repositories. DTOs for input/output. Guards/middleware for auth.
- Centralized error handling, request validation, structured logging with request IDs.
- Graceful shutdown, timeouts and retries (with backoff and jitter) for outbound calls, idempotency for retried operations.
- Don't block the event loop; offload heavy work to queues/workers.

## 6. Python (Django / FastAPI)
- PEP 8, type hints on public functions, docstrings for non-obvious behavior; follow ruff/black/mypy config if present.
- Use virtual envs/uv and pinned dependencies. Don't install packages globally.
- Django:
  - Thin views, logic in services/models/managers. Use forms/serializers for validation.
  - Avoid N+1: `select_related` / `prefetch_related`; use `only()`/`values()` for large reads; paginate.
  - Migrations: never edit applied migrations; one migration per logical change; review generated SQL; make them reversible; avoid long locks on big tables.
  - Bulk data corrections: write idempotent management commands, support `--dry-run`, log counts, use transactions and batching (`iterator()`, `bulk_update`), back up/verify before running on production.
  - Use settings per environment; never hardcode secrets; use `get_object_or_404`, proper permissions and CSRF.
- FastAPI: Pydantic models for I/O, dependency injection for auth/DB/session, async only for truly async I/O, explicit response models and status codes.
- Tests with pytest (pytest-django where applicable), factories/fixtures over ad-hoc data.

## 7. API Design
- Consistent resource naming, correct HTTP verbs/status codes, stable error format (code, message, details, request id).
- Version public APIs; never break contracts silently; deprecate with notice.
- Pagination, filtering, sorting on list endpoints; enforce max page sizes.
- Idempotency keys for payment/create-like operations; rate limiting on public endpoints.
- Document with OpenAPI/Swagger and keep it in sync with code.

## 8. Database & Data Safety
- NEVER run destructive operations (DROP, TRUNCATE, `migrate reset`, `prisma db push --force`, mass DELETE/UPDATE) without explicit confirmation and a backup/rollback plan.
- Backward-compatible schema changes: add → backfill → switch reads/writes → remove.
- Index for frequent filters/joins/foreign keys; verify with EXPLAIN on heavy queries.
- Transactions for multi-step writes; make background jobs idempotent and retry-safe.
- Use parameterized queries/ORM only. No string-built SQL.
- Never use production data in dev/test without anonymization.

## 9. Security (non-negotiable)
- No secrets in code, logs, commits, or examples. Use env vars / secret managers. Keep `.env*` out of git; maintain `.env.example` with placeholders.
- AuthN/AuthZ checked server-side on every protected route and object (prevent IDOR). Least privilege, RBAC.
- Passwords: argon2/bcrypt. JWT: short-lived access tokens, refresh rotation, verify signature/issuer/audience/expiry; prefer httpOnly secure cookies for browser sessions.
- Prevent XSS (escape/sanitize output, CSP), CSRF, SSRF (allowlist outbound hosts), injection, path traversal, open redirects, mass assignment.
- Validate and limit file uploads (type, size, content sniffing), store outside web root, scan if possible.
- Don't log PII, tokens, passwords, or full payment data. Redact in error reports.
- CORS: explicit origins only. Security headers enabled (HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options, frame protections).
- Webhooks: verify signatures, handle replays, respond fast and process async.
- Keep dependencies updated; flag known vulnerabilities (npm audit / pip-audit) and don't ignore them silently.

## 10. Testing
- Every behavior change gets a test. Bug fix = reproduce with a failing test first, then fix.
- Test behavior, not implementation details. Cover happy path, edge cases, error paths, permissions.
- Fast, deterministic, isolated tests. Mock only external boundaries (network, time, payments, LLM calls).
- Pyramid: many unit tests, fewer integration tests, few end-to-end tests for critical flows.
- Don't delete or weaken a failing test to make it pass; fix the cause or explain why the test is wrong.
- Before declaring done, run the project's full check: type-check → lint → tests → build.

## 11. Observability & Error Handling
- Structured logs (JSON) with level, request/correlation ID, user/tenant ID (non-PII), and context.
- Log at the right level; no noisy debug logs in production paths; never log secrets.
- Add health/readiness endpoints, metrics for latency/error rate/queue depth, and alerts on user-impacting failures.
- Return user-safe error messages; keep stack traces server-side.

## 12. Performance
- Measure before optimizing (profiler, EXPLAIN, Lighthouse, load tests). Fix algorithms and queries before micro-optimizations.
- Cache deliberately with clear TTL and invalidation. Avoid caching user-specific data in shared caches.
- Batch and paginate. Avoid unbounded queries, loops with I/O, and loading big files into memory (stream instead).
- Frontend: minimize JS, code-split, optimize images/fonts, avoid layout shift.

## 13. Git & Collaboration
- Work on feature branches; never commit directly to main/production.
- Conventional commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `chore:`, `perf:`, `ci:`). Small, focused commits.
- Don't commit secrets, build artifacts, large binaries, or generated files that aren't tracked by convention.
- NEVER `git push --force`, `git reset --hard`, `git clean -fd`, or delete branches/tags without explicit approval.
- Keep PRs small and reviewable; include summary, testing notes, screenshots for UI, and migration/rollback notes.
- Run pre-commit hooks; don't bypass them with `--no-verify`.

## 14. DevOps, Cloud & Infrastructure as Code
- Always show the plan/diff first (`terraform plan`, `cdk diff`, `kubectl diff`). NEVER apply/deploy to production without explicit confirmation. Prefer staging first.
- Docker: pinned base images, multi-stage builds, non-root user, `.dockerignore`, minimal layers, health checks.
- Separate config per environment (dev/staging/prod) via env vars/parameters; no environment-specific hardcoding.
- AWS: least-privilege IAM, no wildcard permissions without justification, encryption at rest/in transit, private subnets for data stores, tagging, cost awareness, budgets/alarms. Prefer managed services where sensible (ECS/App Runner/RDS/SQS/SNS).
- CI/CD: lint, type-check, test, build, security scan on every PR; deploy from the pipeline, not from laptops; keep rollbacks simple.
- Secrets in Secrets Manager/Parameter Store or CI secrets, never in repo or images.

## 15. AI / LLM Features (LangChain, RAG, agents, chatbots)
- Treat LLM output as untrusted input: validate with schemas, escape before rendering, never execute it directly.
- Guard against prompt injection: separate system/user/retrieved content, don't give the model tools or permissions beyond need, require confirmation for destructive tool actions.
- Set timeouts, retries with backoff, max tokens, and cost/usage limits. Handle provider failures and rate limits (and free-model instability) with fallbacks.
- Keep API keys server-side only. Log prompts/responses carefully with PII redaction.
- Version prompts; keep them in files/config, not scattered strings. Add evals/regression tests with fixed examples for critical behaviors.
- RAG: chunk deliberately, store source metadata, cite sources, and handle "no relevant context" without hallucinating.
- Make model name/provider configurable via env so it can be swapped without code changes.

## 16. Documentation
- Update README, `.env.example`, API docs, and setup/run instructions whenever behavior or configuration changes.
- Record non-obvious decisions in short ADR-style notes (context, decision, consequences).
- Public functions/modules: concise docstrings/JSDoc with inputs, outputs, errors.

## 17. Commands & Tool Safety
- Explore with read-only commands first (`ls`, `grep`, `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`).
- Require explicit approval before: deleting files/directories, overwriting uncommitted work, running migrations on non-local DBs, deploying, publishing packages, force operations, or touching production resources.
- Check whether a dev server/process is already running before starting another. Don't leave stray background processes.
- Don't run unknown scripts from the internet; inspect first. Install dependencies only when needed and say which and why.
- Prefer project scripts (`npm run`, `uv run`, `make`) over ad-hoc commands.

## 18. Definition of Done
- [ ] Requirement met and edge cases handled
- [ ] Type-check, lint, tests, and build pass (or failures are explained)
- [ ] New/changed behavior covered by tests
- [ ] No secrets, debug code, dead code, or stray TODOs added
- [ ] Docs/config/.env.example updated where relevant
- [ ] Migrations/IaC reviewed and reversible; no unconfirmed destructive actions
- [ ] Summary delivered: what changed, how verified, risks, follow-ups

## 19. Communication Style
- Be concise and direct; lead with the outcome or the blocker.
- When offering options, recommend one and give the trade-off in one or two lines.
- State assumptions explicitly. If asked for something unwise, say so briefly and offer a better path instead of silently complying or silently deviating.
- Don't apologize repeatedly or pad answers. Don't over-explain code the user clearly understands.

## 20. PROJECT-SPECIFIC (edit per repo)
- Stack: <e.g., Django + uv + ruff + mypy + pytest-django + pre-commit | Next.js + TypeScript + Prisma + PostgreSQL>
- Install: <command>
- Dev server: <command>
- Test: <command>   Lint: <command>   Type-check: <command>   Build: <command>
- Layout: <e.g., src/<package>/, apps/, deploy/cdk/ (prod & staging configs)>
- Environments: local → staging → production. Staging first for risky changes; production only with explicit approval.
- Do-not-touch: <generated files, vendored code, applied migrations, lockfiles unless asked>
- Conventions: <naming, folder rules, branching, PR template>
